Internationally, "Livin' la Vida Loca" topped the charts in more than 20 countries and is considered to be Martin's biggest hit,<ref name="Billboard Lyrics"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/10281503|title=Ricky Martin lands Broadway Evita role|publisher=[[BBC News]]|access-date=May 21, 2021|date=June 10, 2010|archive-date=May 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521152544/https://www.bbc.com/news/10281503|url-status=live}}</ref> and one of the best-selling singles of all time.<ref name="Rolling Stone 20"/><ref name="Entertainment Tonight 20"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.pastemagazine.com/music/boy-bands/best-solo-albums-boy-bands/#8-ricky-martin-ricky-martin|title=The 10 Best Solo Albums by Former Boy Band Members|last=Boik|first=Daniella|work=[[Paste (magazine)|Paste]]|access-date=May 23, 2021|date=March 10, 2020|archive-date=May 23, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523100700/https://www.pastemagazine.com/music/boy-bands/best-solo-albums-boy-bands/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the United States, the single debuted at number 54 on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] on April 17, 1999, becoming Martin's third entry.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-04-17|title=Billboard Hot 100|magazine=Billboard|date=April 17, 1999|access-date=May 21, 2021|archive-date=May 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521161132/https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-04-17|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="hot100"/> The following week it climbed to number 32,<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-04-24|title=Billboard Hot 100|magazine=Billboard|date=April 24, 1999|access-date=May 21, 2021|archive-date=May 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521161206/https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-04-24|url-status=live}}</ref> and in its third week, it became Martin's first top 10 in the country.<ref name="hot100"/><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-05-01|title=Billboard Hot 100|magazine=Billboard|date=May 1, 1999|access-date=May 21, 2021|archive-date=May 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521161757/https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-05-01|url-status=live}}</ref> In its fourth week, it reached number one and remained at the top for five consecutive weeks,<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-05-08|title=Billboard Hot 100|magazine=Billboard|date=May 8, 1999|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522080733/https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1999-05-08|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/smooth-santana-rob-thomas-hot-100-anniversary/|title=Happy 60th Birthday, Hot 100: Santana & Rob Thomas' 'Smooth' Is Still Your Second-Biggest Song Ever|last=Payne|first=Chris|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 22, 2021|date=August 2, 2017|archive-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522081116/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/chart-beat/8468022/smooth-santana-rob-thomas-hot-100-anniversary|url-status=live}}</ref> making Martin the first solo male artist in over a year to capture the top spot and achieve the first number one hit for his label, Columbia.<ref>{{cite magazine|year=1999|title=C2 Says: Oh, Ricky, You're So Fine|magazine=Billboard|page=86|publisher=Nielsen Business Media|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eQ0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA86|access-date=May 22, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=88|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522085515/https://books.google.com/books?id=eQ0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA86|archive-date=May 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> "Livin' la Vida Loca" became the first number-one song made entirely in Pro Tools.<ref name="Billboard Harley"/> It finished 1999 as the [[Billboard Year-End Hot 100 singles of 1999|year's number ten ''Billboard'' Hot 100 song]] and the decade's 38th top song.<ref name="usend"/> The song reached number one on the US [[Dance Singles Sales|Maxi-Singles Sales]],<ref>{{cite magazine|year=1999|title=Maxi-Singles|magazine=Billboard|page=29|publisher=[[Nielsen Holdings|Nielsen Business Media]]|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eQ0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA29|access-date=May 22, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=88|archive-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eQ0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA86|archive-date=May 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Mainstream Top 40]],<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/pop-songs/1999-05-29|title=Pop Songs|magazine=Billboard|date=May 29, 1999|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=September 24, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190924011101/https://www.billboard.com/charts/pop-songs/1999-05-29|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Adult Top 40]],<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/adult-pop-songs/1999-06-19|title=Adult Pop Songs|magazine=Billboard|date=June 19, 1999|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=September 24, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190924020819/https://www.billboard.com/charts/adult-pop-songs/1999-06-19|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Rhythmic (chart)|Rhythmic Top 40]],<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/rhythmic-40/1999-06-26|title=Rhythmic Songs|magazine=Billboard|date=June 26, 1999|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=June 20, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190620083941/https://www.billboard.com/charts/rhythmic-40/1999-06-26|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Top 40 Tracks]] charts.<ref name="ust40"/> Thus, it became the first song in history to top ''Billboard''{{'}}s Adult Top 40, Mainstream Top 40, and Rhythmic Top 40 charts, holding its record as the only song to do so for 14 years.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/chart-beat/9528266/mood-crossover-airplay-chart-history/|title=24kGoldn & Iann Dior's 'Mood' Makes Crossover History on Radio Airplay Charts|last=Trust|first=Gary|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 22, 2021|date=February 19, 2021|archive-date=February 26, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226013417/https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/chart-beat/9528266/mood-crossover-airplay-chart-history/|url-status=live}}</ref> On the US [[Dance Club Songs|Dance Club Play]] chart, it peaked at number five, becoming Martin's first top 10.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/dance-club-play-songs/1999-06-26|title=Dance Club Songs|magazine=Billboard|date=June 26, 1999|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=September 27, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190927051905/https://www.billboard.com/charts/dance-club-play-songs/1999-06-26|url-status=live}}</ref>

The song was certified platinum by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] (RIAA), denoting shipments of over one million copies.<ref name="RIAA"/> As of February 2000, the song has sold over 1.2&nbsp;million physical copies in the United States, according to [[MRC Data|Nielsen SoundScan]],<ref name="physical">{{cite magazine|year=2000|title=Backstreets, Cher, TLC among Those Up for Record of the Year|magazine=Billboard|page=20|publisher=Nielsen Business Media|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=1A0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA20|access-date=May 22, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=136|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522091731/https://books.google.com/books?id=1A0EAAAAMBAJ&lpg=PA20&pg=PA20|archive-date=May 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> and although it was released years before the [[Digital distribution|digital era]], it has sold over 502,000 digital copies in the United States, as of January 2011.<ref name="digital">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/ask-billboard-what-the-forget-is-going-on/|title=Ask Billboard: Viva la 'Vida' Singer|magazine=Billboard|last=Trust|first=Gary|date=January 28, 2011|access-date=December 15, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150531144752/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/chart-beat/473334/ask-billboard-what-the-forget-is-going-on?page=0%2C1|archive-date=May 31, 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> The Spanish version debuted at number two on ''Billboard''{{'}}s [[Hot Latin Songs|Hot Latin Tracks]] chart on April 17, 1999,<ref>{{cite magazine|year=1999|title=Hot Latin Tracks|magazine=Billboard|page=30|publisher=Nielsen Business Media|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Pg0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA30|access-date=May 22, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=100|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522093959/https://books.google.com/books?id=Pg0EAAAAMBAJ&lpg=PA91&pg=PA30|archive-date=May 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> and reached number one the following week,<ref>{{cite magazine|year=2001|title=For the First Time All His Spanish Language Hits on One Album La Historia|magazine=Billboard|page=80|publisher=Nielsen Business Media|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4BMEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PT2|access-date=April 6, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=80|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210406072117/https://books.google.com/books?id=4BMEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PT2&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjm6NC2iOnvAhWJilwKHdKAATsQ6AEwAHoECAMQAg|archive-date=April 6, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Hot Latin Tracks">{{cite magazine|year=1999|title=Hot Latin Tracks|magazine=Billboard|page=48|publisher=Nielsen Business Media|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eigEAAAAMBAJ&pg=RA1-PA48|access-date=May 22, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=92|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522095104/https://books.google.com/books?id=eigEAAAAMBAJ&lpg=PA7&lr=&rview=1&pg=RA1-PA48|archive-date=May 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> becoming Martin's third number one.<ref name="latinsongs" /> It remained at the top for nine weeks.<ref name="Billboard Latin Dance"/> On May 15, 1999, it became the first single ever to top four different ''Billboard'' charts and made Martin the first act to simultaneously scale a pop, Latin, and dance chart. Two weeks later, he became the first artist to simultaneously top the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]], Hot Latin Tracks, Hot Dance Music/Club Play, Hot Dance Music/Maxi-Singles Sales, Top 40 Tracks, and the ''Billboard'' Hot 100.<ref>{{cite magazine|year=1999|title=Enrique Iglesias/Interscope Deal Likely|magazine=Billboard|page=40|publisher=Nielsen Business Media|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=mg0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA40|access-date=May 25, 2021|issn=0006-2510|volume=104|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210525191457/https://books.google.com/books?id=mg0EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA40&lpg=PA40|archive-date=May 25, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

In November 1999, it was labeled as one of the "hottest tracks" for Sony Discos in a list including the most successful songs released by the label since the launching of the ''Billboard'' Hot Latin Tracks chart in 1986.<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Mayfield|first=Geoff|date= November 20, 1999|title = Charting Sony Discos' Success|magazine =Billboard|publisher =Nielsen Business Media|volume = 111|issue=47|page =74|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fwgEAAAAMBAJ&q=billboard+cosas+del+amor&pg=PA74|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522123113/https://books.google.com/books?id=fwgEAAAAMBAJ&q=billboard+cosas+del+amor&pg=PA74|archive-date=May 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> In January 2010, almost 11 years after the song's release, ''Billboard'' introduced a US [[Latin Digital Songs|Latin Digital Song Sales]] chart and "Livin' la Vida Loca" debuted at number 15 on January 23, 2010, before reaching a peak of number two on September 3, 2011.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/latin-digital-song-sales/2010-01-23|title=Latin Digital Song Sales|magazine=Billboard|date=January 23, 2010|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=October 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201012030639/https://www.billboard.com/charts/latin-digital-song-sales/2010-01-23|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/latin-digital-song-sales/2011-09-03|title=Latin Digital Song Sales|magazine=Billboard|date=September 3, 2011|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522123232/https://www.billboard.com/charts/latin-digital-song-sales/2011-09-03|url-status=live}}</ref> The song has since become Martin's longest-charting hit, spending 357 weeks on the chart.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/artist/ricky-martin/chart-history/ldt/|title=Ricky Martin Chart History (Latin Digital Song Sales)|magazine=Billboard|access-date=October 21, 2021|archive-date=October 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211021144837/https://www.billboard.com/music/ricky-martin/chart-history/LDT|url-status=live}}</ref>

"Livin' la Vida Loca" [[List of number-one singles of 1999 (Canada)|spent eight consecutive weeks]] atop Canada's ''[[RPM (magazine)|RPM]]'' 100 Hit Tracks chart and topped the country's year-end chart.<ref>Citations regarding the weekly chart performance for "Livin' la Vida Loca" in Canada:
