* {{cite web|url=https://recherche-collection-search.bac-lac.gc.ca/eng/home/record?IdNumber=5902&app=rpm|title=RPM 100 Hit Tracks – July 19, 1999|work=RPM|date=July 17, 2013 |publisher=Library and Archives Canada|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=March 31, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190331043228/http://www.bac-lac.gc.ca/eng/discover/films-videos-sound-recordings/rpm/Pages/image.aspx?Image=nlc008388.8383&URLjpg=http%3a%2f%2fwww.collectionscanada.gc.ca%2fobj%2f028020%2ff4%2fnlc008388.8383.gif&Ecopy=nlc008388.8383|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://recherche-collection-search.bac-lac.gc.ca/eng/home/record?IdNumber=5778&app=rpm|title=RPM 1999 Top 100 Hit Tracks|work=RPM|date=July 17, 2013 |publisher=Library and Archives Canada|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=March 29, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190329151600/http://www.bac-lac.gc.ca/eng/discover/films-videos-sound-recordings/rpm/Pages/image.aspx?Image=nlc008388.9952&URLjpg=http%3a%2f%2fwww.collectionscanada.gc.ca%2fobj%2f028020%2ff4%2fnlc008388.9952.gif&Ecopy=nlc008388.9952|url-status=live}}</ref> In the United Kingdom, it debuted at number one and stayed there for three weeks, making Martin the first Puerto Rican artist in history to hit number one.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/charts/singles-chart/19990711/7501/|title=Official Singles Chart Top 100|publisher=[[Official Charts Company]]|access-date=May 22, 2021|archive-date=June 14, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200614200640/https://www.officialcharts.com/charts/singles-chart/19990711/7501/|url-status=live}}</ref> As of May 2017, Martin and [[Luis Fonsi]] are the only Puerto Rican singers to reach number one in the UK.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/luis-fonsi-on-the-evolution-of-despacito-reaching-number-1-across-the-world-and-justin-bieber-interview__19087/|title=Luis Fonsi on the evolution of Despacito, reaching Number 1 across the world and Justin Bieber: Interview|last=Copsey|first=Rob|publisher=Official Charts Company|access-date=May 22, 2021|date=May 12, 2017|archive-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522155132/https://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/luis-fonsi-on-the-evolution-of-despacito-reaching-number-1-across-the-world-and-justin-bieber-interview__19087/|url-status=live}}</ref> Later in 1999, the song was certified platinum by the [[British Phonographic Industry]] (BPI), denoting sales of over 600,000 physical copies.<ref name="UKcertificationP"/> It sold a total of 776,000 copies in the country that year, becoming the sixth best-selling song of 1999. In March 2021, the track was certified [[double platinum]] by the British Phonographic Industry (BPI) for additional track-equivalent sales of 1.2&nbsp;million digital units since 2011.<ref name="UKcertification"/> In Australia, it peaked at number four and was certified double platinum by the [[Australian Recording Industry Association]] (ARIA), denoting shipments of over 140,000 copies.<ref name="au chart"/><ref name="Australia Certification"/> The song reached number one in Argentina,<ref name="Argentina"/> Chile,<ref name="Argentina"/> Costa Rica,<ref name="Puerto Rico"/> El Salvador,<ref name="Puerto Rico"/> Greece,<ref name="Greece"/> Guatemala,<ref name="Guatemala"/> Hungary,<ref name="Hungary"/> Ireland,<ref name="Ireland"/> Mexico,<ref name="Mexico"/> New Zealand,<ref name="New Zealand"/> Nicaragua,<ref name="Puerto Rico"/> Panama,<ref name="Puerto Rico"/> Puerto Rico,<ref name="Puerto Rico"/> and Saudi Arabia.<ref name="Mexico"/> Additionally, it peaked in the top 10 of numerous countries, including France,<ref name="fr chart"/> Germany,<ref name="de chart"/> Italy,<ref name="eu"/> Spain,<ref name="es chart"/> Sweden,<ref name="se chart"/> and Switzerland.<ref name="ch chart"/> In Romania, "Livin' la Vida Loca" was listed as the most-played song of the year.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.rt100.ro/topul-anului-1999.html|title=Topul anului 1999|publisher=[[Romanian Top 100]]|language=ro|access-date=May 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050122082422/http://www.rt100.ro/topul-anului-1999.html|archive-date=January 22, 2005}}</ref>

{{Quote box|width=300px|align=left|quote="The singer's mega hit came out in 1999, and helped turn the late '90s into a Latin pop explosion."|source=—''[[Entertainment Tonight]]''{{'}}s Liz Calvario in 2019.<ref name="Entertainment Tonight 20"/>}}
Martin is regarded by the media as the "[[Honorific nicknames in popular music|Original Latin Crossover King]]".<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-latin/sebastian-yatra-ricky-martin-falta-amor-2020-tour-973951|title=Sebastián Yatra, Ricky Martin Give 'Falta Amor' a Soulful Revamp|last=Exposito|first=Suzy|magazine=Rolling Stone|access-date=May 30, 2021|date=March 26, 2020|archive-date=May 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210530054245/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-latin/sebastian-yatra-ricky-martin-falta-amor-2020-tour-973951/|url-status=live}}</ref> Angie Romero from ''Billboard'' wrote: "If you look up 'crossover' in the dictionary, there should be a photo of Ricky [[Shake Your Bon-Bon|shaking his bon bon]] and/or 'Livin' la Vida Loca'."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/latin/latin-crossover-artists-poll-shakira-ricky-martin-enrique-iglesias-6620381/|title=Shakira, Enrique, Ricky Martin & More: Which Latin Icon Pulled Off the Best Crossover? Vote!|last=Romero|first=Angie|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 30, 2021|date=July 8, 2015|archive-date=May 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210530055328/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/latin/6620381/latin-crossover-artists-poll-shakira-ricky-martin-enrique-iglesias|url-status=live}}</ref> Following his performance of "The Cup of Life" at the Grammys, and the success of "Livin' la Vida Loca" and ''Ricky Martin'' (1999), he opened the gates for many Latin artists such as [[Jennifer Lopez]], [[Shakira]], [[Christina Aguilera]], [[Marc Anthony]], [[Santana (band)|Santana]], and [[Enrique Iglesias]] who released their crossover albums and followed him onto the top of the charts.<ref name="Billboard RM ranked"/><ref name="LA Times"/><ref>Citations from ''Billboard'' regarding "Livin' la Vida Loca"{{'}}s impact:
* {{cite magazine |last1=Fernandez |first1=Suzette |title=Revisiting the Song That Changed Ricky Martin's Career: 'Cup of Life' Turns 20 |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/latin/ricky-martin-cup-of-life-20-years-8238667/ |magazine=Billboard|access-date=June 8, 2020 |date=March 9, 2018|url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200614093036/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/latin/8238667/ricky-martin-cup-of-life-20-years|archive-date=June 14, 2020}}
* {{cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/rewinding-the-charts-in-1999-the-world-went-loca-over-ricky/| title=Rewinding the Charts: In 1999, The World Went 'Loca' Over Ricky Martin|last=Anderson|first=Trevor|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 30, 2021|date=May 8, 2017|archive-date=May 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210530061823/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/chart-beat/7784999/rewinding-the-charts-in-1999-the-world-went-loca-over-ricky|url-status=live}}</ref> According to ''Entertainment Tonight'', "Livin' la Vida Loca" paved the way for a large number of other Latin artists,<ref name="Entertainment Tonight 20"/> and is "credited as the song that helped other Latin artists break through to English-speaking markets".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.etonline.com/gallery/18-latino-chart-toppers-making-us-swoon-113821/ricky-martin-58149| title=18 Latino Chart-Toppers Making Us Swoon|last=Garcia|first=Cynthia| work=Entertainment Tonight|access-date=August 24, 2021|date=March 1, 2021|archive-date=August 24, 2021| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210824144025/https://www.etonline.com/gallery/18-latino-chart-toppers-making-us-swoon-113821/ricky-martin-58149|url-status=live}}</ref>

As believed by ''[[Spin (magazine)|Spin]]'', the song "lit the fuse for the Latin pop explosion of the '90s". Lucas Villa from ''Spin'' wrote about it: "When the world went loca for Ricky, he led the way for other Latin music superstars like Spain's Enrique Iglesias, Colombia's Shakira and Nuyoricans like Jennifer Lopez and Marc Anthony to make their marks beyond the Spanish-speaking crowds."<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.spin.com/2020/11/the-most-influential-artists-27-ricky-martin| title=The Most Influential Artists: #27 Ricky Martin|last=Villa|first=Lucas|work=[[Spin (magazine)|Spin]]| access-date=November 11, 2020 |date=November 10, 2020|archive-date=November 10, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201110061440/https://www.spin.com/2020/11/the-most-influential-artists-27-ricky-martin|url-status=live}}</ref> He described Martin as "a trailblazer in globalizing Latinx culture" in his Grammy.com article.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.grammy.com/news/ricky-martin-need-more-lgbtq-visibility-album-play-feeling-proud-papa-latinx-artists-interview|title=Ricky Martin On The Need For More LGBTQ+ Visibility, Forthcoming Album 'Play' & Feeling Like A 'Proud Papa' To Younger Latinx Artists|last=Villa|first=Lucas|publisher=Grammy Awards|date=June 16, 2021|access-date=June 23, 2021|archive-date=June 23, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210623082855/https://www.grammy.com/grammys/news/ricky-martin-need-more-lgbtq-visibility-album-play-feeling-proud-papa-latinx-artists-interview|url-status=live}}</ref> Also from Grammy.com, Ernesto Lechner described "Livin' la Vida Loca" as "the [[manifesto]] for all the fun-loving, tropically tinged Latin hit singles that followed", stating that Martin led "the Latin music explosion that took over the U.S. at the tail end of the '90s".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.grammy.com/grammys/news/history-puerto-rico-music-salsa-tropical-reggaet%C3%B3n-beyond|title=The History Of Puerto Rico + Music: Salsa, Tropical, Reggaetón & Beyond|last=Lechner|first=Ernesto|publisher=Grammy Awards|date=October 15, 2017|access-date=October 9, 2021|archive-date=October 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211009105412/https://www.grammy.com/grammys/news/history-puerto-rico-music-salsa-tropical-reggaet%C3%B3n-beyond|url-status=dead}}</ref> According to ''[[The Independent]]'', the single is "widely regarded as the song that began the first Latin pop explosion".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/features/black-country-new-road-saxophone-b1855157.html|title=Let's talk about sax, baby: How one of music's most maligned instruments reconquered pop and indie|work=[[The Independent]]|access-date=May 31, 2021|date=May 29, 2021|archive-date=May 31, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210531090908/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/features/black-country-new-road-saxophone-b1855157.html|url-status=live}}</ref> ''[[People (magazine)|People]]''{{'}}s Jason Sheeler credited it as the song that "led the way for the late-'90s so-called 'Latin explosion' that dominated pop music into the new century: Shakira, Enrique Iglesias, Marc Anthony, and Jennifer Lopez".<ref>{{cite news| url=https://people.com/music/ricky-martin-disappointed-by-lack-of-acting-offers| title=Ricky Martin Is 'Disappointed' by His Lack of Acting Offers: 'If It's Because I'm Gay, That's Sad'|last=Sheeler|first=Jason|work=[[People (magazine)|People]]|access-date=June 5, 2021|date=June 3, 2021|archive-date=June 5, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210605115935/https://people.com/music/ricky-martin-disappointed-by-lack-of-acting-offers/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Leila Cobo named the track one of the genre's biggest singles of the past 50 years in his 2021 book ''Decoding "Despacito": An Oral History of Latin Music''.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Decoding "Despacito": An Oral History of Latin Music|last=Cobo|first=Leila|author-link=Leila Cobo|publisher=[[Random House|Knopf Doubleday Publishing Group]]|page=165|year=2021|language=en|isbn=9780593081341}}</ref> She wrote about his impact in "[[Whenever, Wherever]]" chapter: "Ricky Martin's phenomenal success opened the door for a string of Latin artists who waved the flags of their heritage, but who sang in English."<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/shakira-daddy-yankee-decoding-despacito-1143970/| title=Excerpt: Shakira, Daddy Yankee Break Down Breakthrough Hits in New Book|magazine=Rolling Stone|access-date=May 30, 2021|date=March 18, 2021|archive-date=April 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210419062502/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/shakira-daddy-yankee-decoding-despacito-1143970/|url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, she compared Martin's song with "Despacito" in one of her ''Billboard'' articles: "'Livin', like 'Despacito', became not just a global hit but a cultural phenomena that transcended all barriers of language and nationality."<ref name="Billboard Record Grammy"/> In another article, she described it as the song that "ignited the late-'90s Latin explosion".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/latin/ricky-martin-chayanne-revisit-hottest-latin-songs-chart-hits-7534184/|title=Ricky Martin & Chayanne Revisit Their Hottest Latin Songs Chart Hits|magazine=Billboard|last=Cobo|first=Leila|access-date=October 17, 2021|date=October 7, 2016|archive-date=October 17, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211017170209/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/latin/7534184/ricky-martin-chayanne-revisit-hottest-latin-songs-chart-hits/|url-status=live}}</ref> Also from ''Billboard'', Gary Trust wrote: "The song helmed a Latin pop boom in the U.S., with Jenner Lopez, Marc Anthony and others crossing over, as well."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/this-week-in-billboard-chart-history-ricky-martins-loca-leads/|title=This Week In Billboard Chart History: Ricky Martin's 'Loca' Leads Hot 100|last=Trust|first=Gary|magazine=Billboard|access-date=May 31, 2021|date=May 5, 2014|archive-date=May 31, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210531084812/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/chart-beat/6077454/this-week-in-billboard-chart-history-ricky-martins-loca-leads|url-status=live}}</ref> Writing for LiveAbout.com, Bill Lamb credited the song as "the record which kicked off a wave of major Latin performers hitting the pop mainstream".<ref name="LiveAbout Latin Pop"/> During an interview with mitú in 2021, Colombian singer-songwriter [[Maluma]] told the channel about Latin Music globalization: "This has been work that started with Ricky Martin's 'Livin' La Vida Loca' and Shakira like 15 or 20 years ago."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://wearemitu.com/latidomusic/grammy-latin-grammy-awards-committees/|title=Grammy Awards Ending 'Secret Committees', They Remain In-Place For Latin Grammys|last=Villa|first=Lucas|publisher=mitú|access-date=May 31, 2021|date=May 4, 2021|archive-date=May 31, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210531083229/https://wearemitu.com/latidomusic/grammy-latin-grammy-awards-committees/|url-status=live}}</ref> Puerto Rican rapper and singer [[Wisin]] has described its music video as the video of another artist that has impacted him the most.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://historico.elsalvador.com/historico/550457/40-datos-curiosos-en-la-vida-de-wisin.html|title=40 datos curiosos en la vida de Wisin|last=Monge|first=Osmín|language=es|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|access-date=May 31, 2021|date=December 18, 2018|archive-date=May 31, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210531131018/https://historico.elsalvador.com/historico/550457/40-datos-curiosos-en-la-vida-de-wisin.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Colombian singer-songwriter [[J Balvin]] referenced "Livin' la Vida Loca" on his song "Reggaetón" (2018) since Martin "gave as clear a depiction of his actualized artistic dreams".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://genius.com/a/the-pride-of-reggaeton-the-rise-of-j-balvin|title=J Balvin has stayed true to his roots on his journey to the top of the charts.|last=Suarez|first=Gary|publisher=[[Genius (website)|Genius]]|access-date=May 13, 2022|date=February 14, 2019|archive-date=May 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513083851/https://genius.com/a/the-pride-of-reggaeton-the-rise-of-j-balvin|url-status=live}}</ref>

